Error Analysis

Easy activities: Sitting, Lying, Running, Jumping. These have very clear signals, either completely flat or with very strong peaks.
Hard activities: Ascending stairs vs descending stairs. The signal looks almost the same in both directions. The model confuses them about 10 to 15 percent of the time. Also standing in elevator vs moving in elevator is hard because the difference is very small.
The most common mistake in all models is: true label is ascending stairs, predicted label is descending stairs. Statistical features and even CNN cannot easily see the direction of movement.

Limitations

Only 8 subjects in the dataset. The model may not generalize well to new people.
Sensors must be placed at exact body positions as in the original dataset.
The model can only recognize the 19 activities in the dataset.
Not tested in real-time conditions with noisy signals.

Conclusion

I built and compared four models. The most important finding was data leakage. Subject-aware split is necessary for honest evaluation. Deep learning models are better than Random Forest because they read the raw time signal and find temporal patterns. The improved CNN with residual connections and data augmentation gave the best result.
